---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import RainField from "../../components/RainField.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Project — Allegra Farrar"
  description="Adaptive Sensing Design for Advanced Satellite Monitoring and Warning of Tropical Cyclones."
>
  <div class="page-shell">
    <RainField />
    <div class="content-wrap">
      <p class="pill">Research Project</p>
      <h1>
        Adaptive Sensing Design for
        <span class="gradient-text">Advanced Satellite Monitoring</span>
        and Warning of Tropical Cyclones
      </h1>

      <p class="summary">
        Tropical cyclones can intensify and shift course quickly, and a lot of the
        most dangerous behavior happens at scales and timescales that today's
        satellite constellations aren't tuned to catch. This project asks: what
        would it look like if a satellite sensor could adapt — in near real time
        — to focus its attention where a storm is actually doing something
        interesting?
      </p>

      <section class="outline">
        <h2>Project Outline</h2>
        <div class="outline-grid">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Motivation</h3>
            <p>
              Highly localized extreme-weather scenarios (rapid intensification,
              eyewall replacement, sudden track changes) are often under-sampled by
              fixed-schedule satellite observation, which can miss the exact moment
              a storm's structure is changing fastest.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Approach</h3>
            <p>
              Design an adaptive sensing scheme that lets a satellite (or
              constellation) re-task its observation strategy based on evolving
              storm state — trading blanket coverage for smarter, targeted looks
              at the regions and moments that matter most for forecasting and
              warning.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Why it matters</h3>
            <p>
              Better-timed, better-targeted observations feed directly into
              forecast models and warning systems, which is ultimately about
              giving people on the ground more accurate, more timely information
              about a storm that's about to affect them.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Background</h3>
            <p>
              This work builds on a foundation in space engineering and remote
              sensing, plus prior research on Mars entry trajectories under
              atmospheric uncertainty — a different planet, but a similar
              question: how do you act well when the atmosphere won't sit still?
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .summary {
    max-width: 680px;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3.6vw, 2.8rem);
    max-width: 780px;
  }

  .outline {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  .outline-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .outline-grid h3 {
    color: var(--accent-space);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .outline-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
